\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The limit precision is not nearest-neighbor}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Fix #{B\ge 4} and #{m>0}, and set #{\mu=m^2}. The
covariance #{C} of \ref{fdip-00BO} is positive definite. Its
inverse, the precision of the limit law, has a nonzero entry
at some distance at least #{2}.}

\proof{
\p{The eigenvalues of the symmetric circulant #{C} are
#{\lambda_q=\sum_{d=0}^{B-1}C_{0d}\cos(2\pi q d/B)}.
From the series in \ref{fdip-00BO}, the sum over one period
equals #{B} when the frequency differs from #{q} by a
multiple of #{B}, and equals #{0} otherwise. Thus
#{\lambda_q=B\sigma_q} with
#{\sigma_q=\sum_{t\in\mathbb Z}\alpha_{q+tB}}. The terms
#{\alpha_{tB}} vanish for #{t\neq 0}, and #{\alpha_0=1/\mu},
so #{\lambda_0=B/\mu}.}
\p{For #{q=1,\ldots,B-1} set #{z=q/B} and
#{b=m/(2\pi B)}. Then #{z} is not an integer, #{b>0},
and #{z\pm ib} are not integers. Moreover
#{\mu+(2\pi(q+tB))^2=(2\pi B)^2\bigl((t+z)^2+b^2\bigr)}.
\citet{4.22.4}{nist2026dlmf} at argument #{\pi z} reads
##{\csc^2(\pi z)
=\sum_{t\in\mathbb Z}\frac{1}{(\pi z-t\pi)^2}.}
Clearing the factor #{\pi^2} produces
##{\sum_{t\in\mathbb Z}\frac{1}{(z-t)^2}
=\frac{\pi^2}{\sin^2(\pi z)}.}
Absolute convergence permits the reindexing
#{t\mapsto -t}, which replaces #{z-t} by #{t+z}.
The resulting sum is
##{\sum_{t\in\mathbb Z}\frac{1}{(t+z)^2}
=\frac{\pi^2}{\sin^2(\pi z)}.}
The decomposition
##{\frac{1}{k^2\bigl(\mu+(2\pi k)^2\bigr)}
=\frac{1}{\mu}\left(\frac{1}{k^2}
-\frac{4\pi^2}{\mu+(2\pi k)^2}\right)}
and that sum give
##{\sigma_q=\frac1\mu-\frac{4B^2\sin^2(\pi q/B)}{\mu}
\sum_{t\in\mathbb Z}\frac{1}{\mu+(2\pi(q+tB))^2}.}
For a complex number #{w} that is not an integer, the
symmetric truncations
##{s_N(w)=\sum_{t=-N}^{N}\frac{1}{t+w}
=\frac{1}{w}+\sum_{n=1}^{N}\frac{2w}{w^2-n^2}}
satisfy #{\lim_{N\to\infty}s_N(w)=\pi\cot(\pi w)}.
\citet{4.22.3}{nist2026dlmf} at argument #{\pi w} reads
##{\cot(\pi w)=\frac{1}{\pi w}
+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{2w}{\pi(w^2-n^2)},}
and multiplication by #{\pi} equals
#{\lim_{N\to\infty}s_N(w)}. Partial fractions give, for
every positive integer #{N},
##{\sum_{t=-N}^{N}\frac{1}{(t+z)^2+b^2}
=\frac{1}{2ib}\bigl(s_N(z-ib)-s_N(z+ib)\bigr).}
Both #{s_N(z\pm ib)} converge, so the symmetric sums
converge. The summands are positive, and therefore the sum
over #{\mathbb Z} equals
##{\frac{\pi}{2ib}\bigl(\cot(\pi(z-ib))-\cot(\pi(z+ib))\bigr).}
The difference of cotangents has numerator
#{\sin(2\pi ib)=i\sinh(2\pi b)} and denominator
#{(\cosh(2\pi b)-\cos(2\pi z))/2}, so the sum equals
##{\frac{\pi}{b}\frac{\sinh(2\pi b)}{\cosh(2\pi b)-\cos(2\pi z)}.}
Since
#{\frac{\pi}{b}=\frac{2\pi^2 B}{m}} and #{2\pi b=m/B}, the inner sum equals
#{\sinh(m/B)/\bigl(2Bm(\cosh(m/B)-\cos\theta)\bigr)}, where
#{\theta=2\pi q/B}.}
\p{Set #{\delta=m/B}. Substitution produces
##{\sigma_q=\frac{1}{m^2}\left(1
-\frac{2B\sin^2\bigl(\frac{\theta}{2}\bigr)\sinh\delta}
{m(\cosh\delta-\cos\theta)}\right).}
The identity #{2\sin^2\bigl(\frac{\theta}{2}\bigr)=1-\cos\theta} rewrites the
factor in parentheses as
#{(A_0-A_1\cos\theta)/(m(\cosh\delta-\cos\theta))}, with
#{A_0=m\cosh\delta-B\sinh\delta} and
#{A_1=m-B\sinh\delta}. Therefore
##{\frac{1}{\lambda_q}=\frac{m^3}{B}
\frac{\cosh\delta-\cos\theta}{A_0-A_1\cos\theta}.}
At #{\cos\theta=1} the factor #{\cosh\delta-1} cancels, because
#{A_0-A_1=m(\cosh\delta-1)}, and the value equals
#{m^2/B=1/\lambda_0}. The same expression holds for every mode.}
\p{The function #{\sinh u-u} vanishes at #{0} and has
derivative #{\cosh u-1}, so #{\sinh\delta>\delta} and
#{A_1<0}. The denominator #{A_0-A_1\cos\theta} is then at
least #{A_0+A_1}. Set #{u=\frac{\delta}{2}}. The double-angle
identities give
#{A_0+A_1=4B\cosh u\,(u\cosh u-\sinh u)}. The function
#{u\cosh u-\sinh u} vanishes at #{0} and has derivative
#{u\sinh u}, positive for #{u>0}. Thus #{A_0+A_1>0}, every
denominator is positive, and #{\lambda_q>0}.}
\p{The displayed reciprocal is affine in #{\cos\theta} only
if #{A_0=A_1\cosh\delta}. That fails:
#{A_0-A_1\cosh\delta=B\sinh\delta(\cosh\delta-1)>0}.
Clearing the denominator in a comparison with
#{A+D\cos\theta} leaves a polynomial of degree at most #{2}.
A nonzero polynomial of that degree has at most two roots, so
the reciprocal agrees with an affine function for at most two
values of #{\cos\theta}. For #{B\ge 4} the values
#{\cos(2\pi q/B)} include at least three distinct numbers. A
nearest-neighbor circulant has eigenvalues
#{A+D\cos(2\pi q/B)}, so the precision has a nonzero entry
at distance #{2} or greater.}
}
